  "23c5335f-3441-43f0-b93d-7d7f3532ba65": {
   "id": "23c5335f-3441-43f0-b93d-7d7f3532ba65",
   "message": {
    "id": "23c5335f-3441-43f0-b93d-7d7f3532ba65",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1762980917.19,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "There it is: `if (!socket.connected) return;` drops every event during the gap.\n\nsocket.io-client actually buffers emits while disconnected by default (`sendBuffer`), so removing the guard is mostly enough:\n\n```ts\nexport function track(evt: TelemetryEvent) {\n  socket.emit(\"telemetry\", { ...evt, clientTs: Date.now(), seq: nextSeq() });\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- Add a `seq` (monotonic per client) so you can detect gaps and duplicates server-side.\n- The buffer lives in memory. If the student refreshes the tab during a disconnect, those events are gone. For today that's acceptable.\n- Server side, bump the heartbeat:\n\n```js\nconst io = new Server(httpServer, { pingInterval: 15000, pingTimeout: 10000 });\n```\n\nThe default `pingInterval` is 25 s, and with a ~30–40 s idle cutoff on some proxy plus jitter you'll see exactly this flapping."
     ]
    },
